  "editorial_notes": [
    {
      "id": "B40-PPIVOT-SOURCE-001",
      "kind": "empty_if_body_in_illustrative_listing",
      "affected_unit_ids": [
        "r005.hefferon-linear-algebra.unit.file.src.gr.ppivot.tex.lstlisting.source-order.0002"
      ],
      "reader_anchor": "ppivot-source-note-1",
      "text": "A semicolon immediately terminates the pivot-comparison if statement. The following max assignment is unconditional and chooses the last visited row, not necessarily the largest absolute entry.",
      "evidence_sha256": "361b5f26d9a9e0623405da61bfe5d5b6e6c696aaaeb772d42a430c04f2bcc6f6"
    },
    {
      "id": "B40-PPIVOT-SOURCE-002",
      "kind": "worked_answer_power_of_ten",
      "affected_unit_ids": [
        "r005.hefferon-linear-algebra.unit.file.src.gr.ppivot.tex.exercise.source-order.10001.answer.source-order.001"
      ],
      "reader_anchor": "ppivot-source-note-2",
      "text": "For the stated 253 + 2/3, the answer writes .25 times 10^2 = 25. Under its intended two-significant-digit restriction the rounded sum is 250, written .25 times 10^3. The prompt says decimal places while the answer uses significant digits; those are not identical conventions.",
      "evidence_sha256": "361b5f26d9a9e0623405da61bfe5d5b6e6c696aaaeb772d42a430c04f2bcc6f6"
    },
    {
      "id": "B40-PPIVOT-SOURCE-003",
      "kind": "changed_rhs_in_first_reduction_row",
      "affected_unit_ids": [
        "r005.hefferon-linear-algebra.unit.file.src.gr.ppivot.tex.exercise.source-order.10003.answer.source-order.001"
      ],
      "reader_anchor": "ppivot-source-note-3",
      "text": "Exercise 3 starts with right-hand side 1.559, but the supplied rounded reduction changes the unchanged first row to 1.569. This is not rounding to four significant digits. The source is preserved; the complete four-digit worked answer has not been repaired here.",
      "evidence_sha256": "361b5f26d9a9e0623405da61bfe5d5b6e6c696aaaeb772d42a430c04f2bcc6f6"
    },
    {
      "id": "B40-PPIVOT-CONDITION-001",
      "kind": "uniqueness_condition",
      "affected_unit_ids": [
        "r005.hefferon-linear-algebra.unit.file.src.gr.ppivot.tex.exercise.source-order.10005.answer.source-order.001"
      ],
      "reader_anchor": "ppivot-source-note-4",
      "text": "The answer derives the unique solution (1,1,1) by division. Uniqueness requires epsilon not equal to 0 or 5/9. At either exceptional value (1,1,1) is still a solution, but the displayed system has infinitely many solutions.",
      "evidence_sha256": "361b5f26d9a9e0623405da61bfe5d5b6e6c696aaaeb772d42a430c04f2bcc6f6"
    },
    {
      "id": "B40-PPIVOT-SOURCE-004",
      "kind": "rounded_example_starts_from_different_matrix",
      "affected_unit_ids": [
        "r005.hefferon-linear-algebra.unit.file.src.gr.ppivot.tex.exercise.source-order.10005.answer.source-order.001"
      ],
      "reader_anchor": "ppivot-source-note-5",
      "text": "Exercise 5(b) starts from a matrix that is not the preceding system with epsilon = .001: its row-2 and row-3 x coefficients change, and four epsilon-dependent coefficients are ten times too small. Later rounded steps must not be treated as a verified calculation for the stated system.",
      "evidence_sha256": "361b5f26d9a9e0623405da61bfe5d5b6e6c696aaaeb772d42a430c04f2bcc6f6"
    }
  ],
